# This PR;
- Add Filter component.
- Implement filtering by Language, stars and updated date.

## Description

This PR adds a new `onStateChange` API that allows tracking overlay lifecycle events (open, close, unmount) in a standardized way. This API makes it easier for developers to implement common patterns like scroll locking, analytics tracking, and integration with state management libraries without manually tracking overlay state.

**Related Issue:** #N/A (or link to issue if one exists)

## Changes

- Added `onStateChange` function to subscribe to overlay state changes
- Emit events when overlays are opened, closed, or unmounted
- Added error handling to prevent callback errors from breaking functionality
- Added comprehensive test coverage for the new API

## Motivation and Context

Many overlay-kit users currently implement their own overlay state tracking mechanisms for common needs like scroll locking. The `onStateChange` API provides a standardized way to solve this problem while still respecting the single responsibility principle - it only notifies about state changes and leaves the implementation of UI behaviors to the user.

## How Has This Been Tested?

- Added unit tests covering all major functionality:
  - Event emission when overlay opens
  - Event emission when overlay closes
  - Event emission when overlay unmounts
  - Cleanup functionality
  - Error handling in callbacks

## Types of changes

- [x] New feature (non-breaking change which adds functionality)
- [ ] Bug fix (non-breaking change which fixes an issue)
- [ ] Breaking change (fix or feature that would cause existing functionality to change)
- [ ] Documentation update

## Checklist

- [x] I have performed a self-review of my own code.
- [x] My code follows the code style of this project.
- [x] I have added tests that prove my feature works.
- [x] New and existing unit tests pass locally with my changes.
- [x] I have updated the documentation accordingly.
- [x] Any dependent changes have been merged and published in downstream modules.

## Further Comments

The implementation follows the single responsibility principle - it only notifies about state changes without implementing any specific UI behavior (like scroll locking). This allows users to implement their own UI behaviors based on these notifications, keeping the library focused on overlay state management.

### Usage Example

```tsx
import { useEffect } from 'react';
import { overlay } from 'overlay-kit';

function App() {
  useEffect(() => {
    // Subscribe to overlay state changes
    const cleanup = overlay.onStateChange((overlayId, action) => {
      console.log(`Overlay ${overlayId}: ${action}`);
      
      // Example: Control body scroll
      if (action === 'open') {
        document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';
      } else if (action === 'close' || action === 'unmount') {
        document.body.style.overflow = '';
      }
    });
    
    // Cleanup on component unmount
    return cleanup;
  }, []);
  
  return (
    <button onClick={() => overlay.open(/* ... */)}>
      Open Overlay
    </button>
  );
}
```

